__d(function(e,t,i,a,o,n,s){o.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Social Science",textbook:"Social and Political Life \u2014 Textbook for Class VI",chapter_number:24,chapter_title:"Key Elements of a Democratic Government",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are the key elements of a democratic government? Name all five.",back:"The five key elements of a democratic government are: (1) Participation \u2014 citizens actively take part in governance through voting, protests, petitions, and community action; (2) Resolution of Conflict \u2014 government provides peaceful mechanisms (courts, dialogue, negotiations) to settle disagreements; (3) Equality and Justice \u2014 all citizens are treated equally before the law; laws protect the weak from the powerful; (4) Accountability \u2014 elected leaders must answer to the citizens who elected them and can be voted out; (5) Rule of Law \u2014 everyone, including the government, is subject to the same laws. Together, these five elements define what makes a government truly democratic.",memory_hook:"PACER: Participation, Accountability, Conflict resolution, Equality/justice, Rule of law. A democracy must PACE itself according to all five \u2014 skip one and it falters."},{id:"fc02",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is participation in a democracy? Give three examples beyond voting.",back:"Participation in a democracy means citizens actively engaging in governance \u2014 not just voting once in five years but continuously influencing decisions. Beyond voting: (1) Peaceful protests and rallies \u2014 citizens gather to express opinions on government policies (e.g., protests against a factory polluting a river); (2) Signing petitions \u2014 collecting signatures to demand government action on an issue; (3) Writing to representatives \u2014 contacting your MLA or MP about local problems; (4) Right to Information (RTI) \u2014 filing RTI applications to get information about government actions; (5) Community meetings \u2014 attending Gram Sabha (village assembly) to participate in local decision-making; (6) Running for office \u2014 standing as a candidate in local elections. Participation strengthens democracy \u2014 a government that only responds to votes every five years is not fully democratic.",memory_hook:"PARTICIPATION = DEMOCRACY'S FUEL. Without citizens' active engagement, democracy runs on empty. Vote = minimum participation. Protest, petition, RTI, community meeting = full participation. The more citizens participate, the more responsive the government."},{id:"fc03",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is accountability in a democratic government? Why is it essential?",back:"Accountability means that elected leaders and government officials must be answerable for their decisions and actions to the citizens who elected/appointed them. Why essential: (1) Without accountability, leaders act in self-interest rather than public interest; (2) Accountability prevents corruption \u2014 officials who must answer for how they spend public money are less likely to misuse it; (3) It ensures policies reflect people's needs \u2014 leaders who must answer to voters are motivated to solve actual problems; (4) It allows course-correction \u2014 if a policy fails, accountable leaders must explain and change it. Mechanisms of accountability: elections (vote out bad leaders), Parliament's question hour (MPs question ministers), RTI Act (citizens demand information), independent media (expose corruption), independent courts (check illegal government actions).",memory_hook:"ACCOUNTABILITY = GOVERNMENT ON TRIAL BEFORE CITIZENS. Every election is a trial: 'What did you do with our votes and taxes?' Leaders who cannot answer are voted out. Leaders who answer well are re-elected. The ballot is the verdict."},{id:"fc04",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the Right to Information (RTI) Act? How does it promote accountability?",back:"The Right to Information (RTI) Act, 2005 gives every Indian citizen the right to request information from any public authority (government department, public sector company, government-funded institution). The government must respond within 30 days. If refused, citizens can appeal to Information Commissioners. RTI promotes accountability because: (1) Citizens can check how public money is spent; (2) Government officers who misuse funds or make arbitrary decisions know citizens can find out; (3) It has exposed corruption \u2014 RTI has revealed inflated contracts, ghost beneficiaries in schemes, and irregular appointments; (4) It gives power to ordinary people to question powerful institutions. The RTI Act is called 'the most powerful weapon of ordinary citizens against corruption.'",memory_hook:"RTI = RIGHT TO INTERROGATE GOVERNMENT. You pay taxes \u2192 government spends them \u2192 RTI lets you ask HOW. 30 days to reply. Government must answer. Sunlight (information) is the best disinfectant for corruption."},{id:"fc05",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How does a government resolve conflicts in a democracy?",back:"Conflicts \u2014 between individuals, communities, states, or citizens and government \u2014 are inevitable in a diverse society. Democratic government resolves them through: (1) Courts \u2014 the judicial system provides a formal, structured way to settle legal disputes; Supreme Court/High Courts/District Courts handle different levels; (2) Police and law enforcement \u2014 intervene in situations of violence to restore order; (3) Government negotiations and dialogue \u2014 when communities have competing interests (e.g., water sharing between states), the government mediates; (4) Legislation \u2014 Parliament passes laws that set clear rules resolving recurring conflicts (e.g., laws on land ownership, inheritance, marriage); (5) Ombudsman/Lokayukta \u2014 special offices that hear citizen grievances against government. The key is that conflicts are settled peacefully through defined processes, not through violence.",memory_hook:"CONFLICT IN DEMOCRACY = DISAGREEMENT + PEACEFUL RESOLUTION. Like a referee in football \u2014 the government referees disputes. Without the referee, players fight. Without government, communities fight. The rule is the same: follow the process, not the fist."},{id:"fc06",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the Lokayukta? How does it function in Karnataka?",back:"The Lokayukta is an independent ombudsman (anti-corruption watchdog) institution in Indian states, responsible for investigating complaints of corruption and misconduct against state government officials, including ministers and MLAs. Karnataka's Lokayukta: established in 1984 under the Karnataka Lokayukta Act \u2014 one of India's first and most active state Lokayuktas. Functions: receives complaints from citizens about government corruption; conducts independent investigations; can recommend prosecution of corrupt officials; submits reports to the Governor and State Legislature. Karnataka's Lokayukta has conducted high-profile raids on government offices and exposed corruption in mining, public works, and education departments. The Lokayukta is an accountability mechanism \u2014 it checks the executive (government) independently.",memory_hook:"LOKAYUKTA = KARNATAKA'S ANTI-CORRUPTION WATCHDOG. Citizens go to Lokayukta when government officers are corrupt. Loka (people) + Ayukta (commissioner) = People's Commissioner. Karnataka established it in 1984 \u2014 now it's a model for the country."},{id:"fc07",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why is equality and justice a key element of democratic government?",back:"Equality and justice are foundational to democracy because: (1) Democracy's promise is that every citizen is equal \u2014 one person, one vote; equal rights before law; (2) Without equality, some citizens have more power than others \u2014 weakening the democratic principle; (3) Justice ensures that the law protects everyone, especially the vulnerable \u2014 the poor, minorities, women, Dalits \u2014 who might otherwise be dominated by the powerful; (4) When citizens feel the system is unfair, they lose faith in democracy and may turn to illegal means; (5) Equality allows diverse communities to coexist \u2014 when all are treated fairly, conflict is minimised. India's Constitution embeds equality (Article 14 \u2014 equality before law; Article 15 \u2014 no discrimination; Article 17 \u2014 abolish untouchability) and justice (the Preamble promises 'social, economic, and political justice').",memory_hook:"EQUALITY = SAME SCALE FOR ALL. Like a weighing scale \u2014 justice is blind (same rules for rich and poor). Democracy without equality = some people have a heavier thumb on the scale. That's not democracy \u2014 that's oligarchy in disguise."},{id:"fc08",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the Gram Sabha? Why is it called the most direct form of democracy?",back:"The Gram Sabha is the general assembly of all adult voters in a Gram Panchayat (village). It is the foundational unit of Indian democracy at the grassroots. Every adult voter in the village is automatically a member \u2014 there is no election to join the Gram Sabha. The Gram Sabha meets at least twice a year (more often in active panchayats) to: discuss village plans and budgets; identify beneficiaries for government schemes; review accounts and audit panchayat spending; raise local issues. Why most direct: unlike Parliament (representatives decide for you), the Gram Sabha is direct democracy \u2014 every adult participates in decisions about their own village. It is India's most direct democratic institution, where a farmer, a woman, and a landowner have the same vote on village decisions.",memory_hook:"GRAM SABHA = VILLAGE'S OWN PARLIAMENT WHERE EVERYONE IS AN MP. No election needed \u2014 if you're an adult voter of the village, you belong. The Gram Sabha is democracy at its most direct: you decide your village's future with your own voice."},{id:"fc09",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a protest? Is protesting legal in India?",back:"A protest is a form of public expression where citizens demonstrate opposition to or demand for a government action, policy, or decision. Yes, peaceful protests are a fundamental right in India: Article 19(1)(b) guarantees the right to assemble peacefully and without arms. Article 19(1)(a) guarantees freedom of speech and expression. Forms of protest: rallies and marches, sit-ins (dharna), strikes, fasts, demonstrations, petition drives, boycotts. Protests are a democratic safety valve \u2014 they allow citizens to express grievances without resorting to violence. Famous Indian protests: Salt March (1930 \u2014 Gandhi), Chipko Movement (1970s \u2014 hugging trees against deforestation), Anna Hazare's anti-corruption fast (2011). Limitations: protests cannot obstruct public order or incite violence \u2014 reasonable restrictions apply.",memory_hook:"PROTEST = DEMOCRACY SPEAKING LOUDLY. When normal channels (RTI, elections) don't work fast enough, citizens protest. Article 19 = right to speak (speech) + right to gather (assembly). Peaceful protest = legal, protected, essential. Violent protest = not protected, not democratic."},{id:"fc10",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How does the media contribute to democratic accountability?",back:"A free press and independent media contribute to democracy by: (1) Informing citizens \u2014 people need accurate information to make democratic decisions; (2) Investigating and exposing corruption \u2014 investigative journalism holds officials accountable when formal mechanisms fail; (3) Providing a platform for debate \u2014 media allows different viewpoints to be heard; (4) Monitoring government \u2014 reporting on government actions, policies, and their impact creates public scrutiny; (5) Giving voice to the marginalised \u2014 covering stories of communities whose issues might otherwise be ignored. The press is called the 'Fourth Estate' \u2014 the fourth pillar of democracy alongside Legislature, Executive, and Judiciary. Freedom of press (not explicitly stated in the Constitution but derived from Article 19) is essential to democratic accountability. When media is controlled by government or corporate interests, accountability suffers.",memory_hook:"MEDIA = DEMOCRACY'S MIRROR AND WATCHDOG. Mirror: shows citizens what the government is doing. Watchdog: barks (reports) when the government misbehaves. A democracy without free media is like a house with no mirrors \u2014 you can't see how you look."},{id:"fc11",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What was the South African anti-apartheid struggle? How does it illustrate democratic principles?",back:"Apartheid was South Africa's system of racial segregation and discrimination (1948\u20131994) under which the white minority government denied Black, Coloured, and Indian citizens basic rights \u2014 separate schools, hospitals, public spaces; no voting rights; forced removals to 'homelands.' Nelson Mandela and the African National Congress (ANC) fought against apartheid through: peaceful protests, international boycotts, and eventually armed resistance. Mandela was imprisoned for 27 years. In 1994, South Africa held its first democratic election \u2014 all citizens regardless of race voted; Mandela became President. This illustrates: (1) Democracy requires equality \u2014 voting rights cannot be denied by race; (2) Participation \u2014 the anti-apartheid struggle was mass participation in the fight for democracy; (3) Justice \u2014 the system changed when international pressure and internal resistance forced the government to account for its injustice.",memory_hook:"SOUTH AFRICA'S LESSON: Democracy delayed is democracy denied. Mandela waited 27 years in prison \u2014 but democracy arrived. Apartheid said: your skin colour determines your rights. Democracy said: your humanity determines your rights. Democracy won."},{id:"fc12",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the difference between a majority decision and respecting minority rights in a democracy?",back:"Majority decision: in a democracy, when people vote (in elections or in Parliament), the majority view wins. This is the basic mechanism of democratic decision-making \u2014 more than half of representatives (or voters) must agree for a decision to pass. Minority rights: but democracy does NOT mean the majority can do anything it wants to the minority. Even if 60% of Parliament votes for a law that denies rights to a religious minority, the courts can strike it down as unconstitutional. India's Constitution specifically protects minority rights: Article 29\u201330 (cultural and educational rights of minorities); Article 25 (freedom of religion). True democracy balances majority rule with minority rights \u2014 the majority decides most things, but cannot take away the fundamental rights of any group. This balance is called 'constitutional democracy' or 'liberal democracy.'",memory_hook:"MAJORITY RULES, BUT CANNOT OVERRIDE RIGHTS. Like a class vote: if 25 out of 30 students vote to bully the remaining 5 \u2014 the majority has voted but done something wrong. Rights protect the 5 from the majority of 25. That's constitutional democracy."},{id:"fc13",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How does the Parliament's 'Question Hour' promote accountability?",back:"Question Hour is the first hour of every Parliamentary session (both Lok Sabha and Rajya Sabha) during which MPs question ministers about government policies, decisions, and actions. Types: Starred Questions (oral answer required \u2014 supplementary questions can follow), Unstarred Questions (written answer only), Short Notice Questions (urgent matters). Significance for accountability: (1) Ministers must prepare and defend their department's decisions in public; (2) MPs can follow up with supplementary questions to probe further; (3) The entire process is televised and reported \u2014 creating public pressure on ministers; (4) Exposes government inaction, corruption, or policy failures. India's Question Hour is one of the most robust accountability mechanisms in its democracy. Karnataka's Vidhan Sabha also has Question Hour \u2014 MLAs question Karnataka's ministers about state matters.",memory_hook:"QUESTION HOUR = PARLIAMENT'S DAILY ACCOUNTABILITY SESSION. For one hour every day, ministers must face MPs' questions in public. No hiding. No delay. The minister who cannot answer is embarrassed publicly. That public embarrassment is accountability in action."},{id:"fc14",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the NCERT chapter's example of democratic decision-making through the story of Neerja and her village? What does it illustrate?",back:"The NCERT chapter uses the story of a village facing water shortage \u2014 the village decides through a Gram Sabha meeting how to manage and distribute the limited water fairly. The Gram Sabha (all adult voters) meets, different community members speak (including women who traditionally might have been excluded), and a collective decision is reached through open discussion and majority agreement. This story illustrates: (1) Participation \u2014 everyone has a voice, not just men or upper castes; (2) Equality \u2014 each person's view is heard equally; (3) Conflict resolution \u2014 competing interests (farmers who want more water for irrigation vs. households who need drinking water) are resolved peacefully through discussion; (4) Local governance \u2014 the Gram Sabha makes a decision relevant to their own community without waiting for state or central government. It shows democracy at the grassroots level.",memory_hook:"GRAM SABHA STORY = DEMOCRACY MADE VISIBLE. Not abstract \u2014 real people, real problem (water), real decision-making process. Every element of democracy \u2014 participation, equality, conflict resolution, accountability \u2014 visible in one village meeting."},{id:"fc15",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why is it important for women to participate in democratic processes?",back:"Women's participation in democracy is essential because: (1) Women are 50% of the population \u2014 decisions made without their participation ignore half of society's needs; (2) Women face specific issues \u2014 maternal health, domestic violence, workplace discrimination \u2014 that are best understood and advocated for by women themselves; (3) Women's participation changes policy priorities \u2014 research shows legislatures with more women pass more social welfare legislation; (4) It models equality \u2014 women in leadership positions challenge gender stereotypes; (5) Democratic legitimacy \u2014 a government that excludes women from participation is not fully democratic. India's measures for women's participation: 33% reservation for women in Panchayati Raj institutions (73rd Amendment) and Urban Local Bodies (74th Amendment); women's Self-Help Groups (SHGs) as a political base in rural areas. Karnataka has implemented 50% reservation for women in Gram Panchayats.",memory_hook:"HALF THE POPULATION = HALF THE PARLIAMENT (ideally). A democracy that silences women is only half a democracy. Karnataka gives 50% Gram Panchayat seats to women \u2014 more than the national requirement. When women lead at the village level, the village level works better."},{id:"fc16",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is transparency in government? How is it achieved?",back:"Transparency means government actions, decisions, and use of public resources are open and visible to citizens \u2014 people can see what the government is doing and why. Transparency is achieved through: (1) RTI Act \u2014 citizens can request any government information; (2) Open government data \u2014 government websites publish budgets, scheme data, contracts; (3) Public hearings \u2014 before major decisions (infrastructure projects), public consultations are held; (4) Social audits \u2014 communities review government spending in their area (especially under MGNREGA, the rural employment guarantee scheme); (5) Independent media reporting on government actions; (6) Parliamentary debates \u2014 televised, accessible to all citizens; (7) Court orders requiring government to disclose. Transparency is the foundation of accountability \u2014 you cannot hold someone accountable for what you cannot see.",memory_hook:"TRANSPARENCY = GLASS WALLS ON GOVERNMENT. When government works in glass (visible), it behaves better than when it works behind opaque walls (hidden). Corruption loves darkness. Transparency = LIGHT. RTI is the switch that turns on the light."},{id:"fc17",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the MGNREGA? How does it embody democratic principles?",back:"MGNREGA (Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act, 2005) guarantees every rural household 100 days of paid work per year on public works projects (road construction, water conservation, afforestation). Democratic principles it embodies: (1) Participation \u2014 workers apply for work; communities decide local works through Gram Sabha; (2) Accountability \u2014 job cards issued to every worker; wages must be paid in 15 days; failure to provide work triggers unemployment allowance; (3) Transparency \u2014 social audits where communities publicly review MGNREGA expenditure; citizens check if money was actually spent on work done; (4) Equality \u2014 every adult rural household eligible regardless of caste or gender; (5) Justice \u2014 provides economic security to the poorest \u2014 Dalits, Adivasis, women \u2014 who most need it. Karnataka implements MGNREGA through Zila Panchayats with significant Dalit and women participation.",memory_hook:"MGNREGA = DEMOCRACY AS DAILY BREAD. Not just abstract rights \u2014 100 days of guaranteed work, paid wages, social audit, job card. Every element of democratic accountability built into one employment scheme. The worker is a rights-holder, not a charity recipient."},{id:"fc18",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the role of political opposition in a democracy?",back:"In a democracy, the political party or coalition that does not win the majority forms the Opposition. The Opposition's democratic roles: (1) Check on the government \u2014 questions government policies, exposes failures, demands accountability in Parliament; (2) Alternative policies \u2014 proposes different solutions, giving citizens a choice; (3) Prevent abuse of power \u2014 a strong opposition makes it harder for the government to act arbitrarily; (4) Represent minorities \u2014 often voices the interests of communities whose preferred party is not in power; (5) Leader of Opposition \u2014 recognised constitutional role; in India, the Leader of Opposition is consulted in appointments to key constitutional bodies (Election Commission, CBI). A democracy without a strong opposition becomes de facto one-party rule \u2014 weakening accountability. India's constitution recognises this by giving the Leader of Opposition official status and salary.",memory_hook:"OPPOSITION = DEMOCRACY'S IMMUNE SYSTEM. Like antibodies that fight disease, the opposition fights government overreach. A government with no opposition gets sick (corrupt). A government that fears a strong opposition stays healthy. Opposition is not the enemy \u2014 it is the check."},{id:"fc19",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How does the social audit of MGNREGA work in Karnataka? Why is it important?",back:"Social audit of MGNREGA in Karnataka: (1) The Social Audit Unit (SAU) of Karnataka \u2014 an independent government body \u2014 conducts audits of MGNREGA works in Gram Panchayats; (2) Local auditors (trained community members) visit work sites and interview workers to verify: were workers actually employed? were wages paid on time? was money spent on actual work? were materials purchased at fair prices? (3) Findings are read out publicly at a Gram Sabha where all villagers can attend; (4) Workers, contractors, and panchayat officials must answer questions about discrepancies; (5) Anomalies are referred to district authorities for action. Why important: social audits are participatory accountability \u2014 citizens themselves check whether government money reached them. It is the most direct form of holding government accountable. Karnataka's SAU has exposed crores in MGNREGA irregularities.",memory_hook:"SOCIAL AUDIT = COMMUNITY BECOMES THE AUDITOR. Instead of a distant government department checking, the community itself checks whether the scheme worked for them. The people who should benefit audit whether they actually did. Accountability by the beneficiaries themselves."},{id:"fc20",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How does Karnataka's Gram Panchayat system embody the five elements of democratic government?",back:"Karnataka's Gram Panchayat system and the five democratic elements: (1) Participation \u2014 Gram Sabha (all adult voters) meets at least twice/year; ward meetings involve smaller groups; women have 50% reservation; (2) Accountability \u2014 Gram Panchayat president and members elected every 5 years; Gram Sabha reviews accounts; social audits of schemes; (3) Conflict resolution \u2014 Gram Nyayalaya (village court) handles minor disputes at the village level; Panchayat mediates water and land disputes; (4) Equality and Justice \u2014 SC/ST and women reservations in panchayat seats ensure marginalised communities have representation; MGNREGA provides economic justice; (5) Rule of Law \u2014 panchayats function under Karnataka Gram Panchayat Act; Gram Panchayat decisions can be challenged in courts. Karnataka's 6,016 Gram Panchayats are the foundation of the state's democratic governance.",memory_hook:"GRAM PANCHAYAT = ALL 5 DEMOCRATIC ELEMENTS IN ONE INSTITUTION. Participation (Gram Sabha), Accountability (elections + audit), Conflict resolution (Nyayalaya), Equality (reservations), Rule of law (Karnataka GP Act). Study your own Gram Panchayat to understand all five elements live."}]}},2933,[]);